---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Digital Marketing AI Worker | Umar Ehtisham">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width: 680px;">

    <a href="/#projects" class="back-link" style="margin-top: 24px;"><svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M19 12H5"/><path d="m12 19-7-7 7-7"/></svg> Back to projects</a>

    <div class="project-header">
      <p class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--teal);">Platforms · LangGraph · In progress</p>
      <h1>Digital Marketing AI Worker</h1>
      <p class="client">An internal multi-tenant product being built at Bigello Digital, planned for external client licensing</p>
    </div>

    <div class="project-body">
      <h3>The problem</h3>
      <p>Marketing teams create and publish social content manually across Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, and X — slow, inconsistent, and hard to scale across multiple client brands at once. Digital Marketing AI Worker is being built to solve this as a proper multi-tenant SaaS product: a chat-first AI worker that takes a topic (or a draft caption, or an image) and turns it into a reviewed, on-brand, scheduled post — with a human always in the loop before anything goes live.</p>

      <h3>Architecture — completed phases</h3>
      <svg width="100%" height="200" viewBox="0 0 680 200" role="img" aria-label="Flow diagram: input goes through the Social Media sub-graph with human-in-the-loop review, then fans out via map-reduce to Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, and X">
        <line x1="80" y1="100" x2="160" y2="100" stroke="#3A3D45" stroke-width="1"/>
        <line x1="320" y1="100" x2="520" y2="26" stroke="#3A3D45" stroke-width="1"/>
        <line x1="320" y1="100" x2="520" y2="74" stroke="#3A3D45" stroke-width="1"/>
        <line x1="320" y1="100" x2="520" y2="122" stroke="#3A3D45" stroke-width="1"/>
        <line x1="320" y1="100" x2="520" y2="170" stroke="#3A3D45" stroke-width="1"/>

        <rect x="10" y="82" width="70" height="36" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#262A31"/>
        <text x="45" y="104" text-anchor="middle" fill="#9AA0AC" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="10">topic</text>

        <rect x="160" y="68" width="160" height="64" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#3FA796"/>
        <text x="240" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="#3FA796" font-family="Space Grotesk, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="500">social media</text>
        <text x="240" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="#6B7080" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9">sub-graph + HITL</text>

        <rect x="520" y="10" width="90" height="32" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#262A31"/>
        <text x="565" y="30" text-anchor="middle" fill="#9AA0AC" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="10">Facebook</text>
        <rect x="520" y="58" width="90" height="32" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#262A31"/>
        <text x="565" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#9AA0AC" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="10">Instagram</text>
        <rect x="520" y="106" width="90" height="32" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#262A31"/>
        <text x="565" y="126" text-anchor="middle" fill="#9AA0AC" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="10">LinkedIn</text>
        <rect x="520" y="154" width="90" height="32" rx="6" fill="#1C1F26" stroke="#262A31"/>
        <text x="565" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#9AA0AC" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="10">X</text>
      </svg>
      <p style="font-size: 13px; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 8px;">Publish Engine (LangGraph Send map-reduce) fans out the approved post to each platform; Celery + Redis distribute the jobs</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Publish Engine (LangGraph Map-Reduce)</strong> — the core publishing layer, built first because it's the riskiest part. Uses LangGraph's Send API to fan out one publish branch per platform in parallel, with a state reducer merging results back into a single report. Transient failures retry with backoff; auth/validation failures fail fast — so one broken platform credential never stalls the others.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Foundation (async job infrastructure)</strong> — Celery + Redis handle job distribution so no client's content generation blocks another's. Postgres holds tenant state, publish reports, and scheduled posts. Multi-tenancy is enforced from the ground up: every request resolves a <code>tenant_id</code> that propagates through the entire pipeline. Real-time updates are pushed to the client via SSE.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Social Media sub-graph (LangGraph)</strong> — the content generation pipeline itself: analyzes whether the input is a topic (create mode) or a draft caption (improve mode), generates caption + hashtags with brand context injected into every LLM call, generates or accepts an image, and runs the result through staged human-in-the-loop review (caption → image → final) before scheduling or publishing. Approved content activates the Publish Engine, either immediately or on a per-platform schedule.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A decision worth naming: separating <em>what</em> gets published (the Map-Reduce publish engine) from <em>when</em> it gets published (a scheduler activated only after final HITL approval) — keeping platform-fan-out logic and scheduling logic independent made both easier to test and extend.</p>

      <h3>Stack</h3>
      <div class="stack-line">
        <span class="tag">FastAPI</span>
        <span class="tag">LangGraph (Send API, sub-graphs, HITL)</span>
        <span class="tag">Celery</span>
        <span class="tag">Redis</span>
        <span class="tag">PostgreSQL</span>
        <span class="tag">SSE</span>
        <span class="tag">Meta Graph API</span>
        <span class="tag">LinkedIn API v2</span>
        <span class="tag">X API v2</span>
      </div>

      <h3>Roadmap — in progress</h3>
      <p>Authentication and multi-tenant session security, a Next.js chat frontend with brand context and knowledge base management, and a WhatsApp channel as a second entry point alongside the web app. A second content sub-graph (long-form blog posts) is planned using the same sub-graph pattern once the first is proven in production.</p>

      <h3>Outcome</h3>
      <p>The publishing core and content generation pipeline are built and functioning end-to-end for social media posts; the product is actively being extended toward full multi-channel, multi-tenant readiness.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</Layout>
